      "Real color enhancement for a water-based acrylic",
      "Low odor, safe to use beside the house",
      "Soap-and-water cleanup",
      "Labeled for pavers, stamped and broom-finished concrete",
      "Sold in sizes that suit small and large patios"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Cannot be re-melted if it dries hazy",
      "Needs warm, dry weather to cure clear",
      "Gloss is a step below solvent wet-look"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The closest a water-based acrylic gets to a solvent wet look, for people who want color pop without the fumes.",
    "description": "WB25 is Foundation Armor's water-based acrylic for people who want the darker, richer wet-look color but do not want the solvent smell or cannot buy solvent products in their state. On concrete pavers it deepens the color noticeably and leaves a satin-to-semigloss sheen, a little less glassy than the company's solvent AR350. Cleanup is water, and the odor is mild enough to use next to open windows. The label asks for thin coats, a clean and dry surface, and moderate temperatures, and that last part is not optional. Water-based acrylic needs warmth and low humidity to fuse into a clear film, and if it is applied heavy or on a damp day it can dry milky. Unlike solvent acrylic, a cured water-based film will not re-dissolve, so mistakes usually mean stripping. Owners commonly report good results when they apply two light coats in the afternoon after a dry spell."
  },
