        <div id="by-finish" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing by Finish, Traffic and Climate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the look. If you want the patio to look freshly rained on, pick a water-based wet-look acrylic like WB25 and accept that it will need a maintenance coat every couple of years. If you want the pavers enhanced without shine, a satin product like Blok-Guard or SB-6000 fits. If you want no visible change at all, choose a matte acrylic like Techniseal's or a penetrating repellent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, think about traffic. Patios that see dining chairs and bare feet are kind to a film. Driveways are not: hot tires turning in place lift and scuff acrylic films, and snow shovels scrape them. On paver driveways, a penetrating repellent or a penetrating enhancer will look more even after a few years than a film that has worn off in the tracks but not along the edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate decides how much freeze-thaw protection you need. In places with real winters, water inside a paver freezes and expands repeatedly, and de-icing salt adds chemical stress on top. A penetrating repellent is the strongest defense there. In hot, dry climates, UV and heat matter more; choose a non-yellowing acrylic and expect sun-exposed areas to need recoating sooner than shaded ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the joints. Full, firm joints of polymeric sand do not need a stabilizing sealer, and a penetrant on the faces is enough. Ordinary sand joints that wash out after every storm benefit from a joint-stabilizing acrylic flooded into them. If the joints are loose, low and full of weeds, fix them first; no sealer can rescue a joint that is half empty.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK MATCH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wet look: WB25. Natural: Techniseal Matte. Loose sand: SB-6000. Snow and salt: Siloxa-Tek 8500.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Eagle Natural Seal is the budget fallback when you only need water repellency, and Blok-Guard suits busy patios where chalk, markers and spills are the main problem.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="weather-window" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Weather Window Is Part of the Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based acrylics do not dry so much as coalesce. Water leaves the film and the resin particles fuse together. If the air is cold, the particles do not soften enough to fuse completely. If the air is humid, the water leaves too slowly and can get trapped. In both cases the result can be a soft or milky film. That is why labels for these products are specific about temperature and humidity in a way solvent labels often are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual target is air and surface temperatures between about 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, rising during application rather than falling, with no rain or heavy dew expected for 24 hours. Late morning to mid-afternoon on a dry day is ideal. Avoid evenings in spring and fall: the temperature drops, dew forms on the fresh film, and you get exactly the haze you were trying to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface temperature matters as much as air temperature. Dark pavers in direct summer sun can run 30 or more degrees hotter than the air, hot enough that the sealer skins over before it levels, leaving roller marks and bubbles. On hot days, work the shaded side first and wait until the sun moves off the rest, or seal early and finish before the pavers heat up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture inside the paver is the hidden variable. A paver that looks dry can still be releasing water from below, especially after a wash or a rainy week, and that vapor pushing up through a fresh water-based film is a common cause of whitening. After washing, give it at least 24 hours of dry weather, 48 in cool or humid conditions. The plastic-sheet test, taping a square of plastic down overnight and checking for condensation, takes the guesswork out.
          </p>

