        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying a Water-Based Sealer Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean first. Remove oil, rust, leaf stains and efflorescence with the right cleaner, then pressure wash with a surface cleaner attachment at moderate pressure so you do not etch the pavers or blast out every joint. Refill the joints with sand to just below the paver chamfer and sweep the faces completely clean. Sand grains left on the surface get glued into the film and feel gritty forever.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a pump sprayer with a fan tip, or a roller for small areas. Spray a manageable section, a few feet wide, and immediately back-roll with a medium-nap roller to push sealer into the texture and spread any puddles. If you are stabilizing joint sand, flood the joints on this first coat until the sealer stops soaking in. Work toward your exit so you never step on fresh sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the coat thin on the paver faces. The most common water-based failure, a milky or white film, comes from a coat that is too thick for the water to escape. Many labels call for two thin coats rather than one heavy one, with the second applied once the first is dry to the touch, often an hour or two in warm weather. Read the recoat window on the label; waiting too long can cause poor adhesion between coats on some products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it cure before you use the patio. Light foot traffic is usually fine after a few hours, but furniture, grills and planters should wait 24 to 48 hours, and cars on a paver driveway longer still. Turn off sprinklers for at least a day. If the pavers are near a pool, keep splash-out off the fresh coat. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">installing polymeric sand</a> explains how long new poly sand needs before any sealer goes on top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compatibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sealing Over Old Coatings and Polymeric Sand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before sealing an older patio, find out what is already on it. Splash some water on the pavers. If it beads on a dry, untreated-looking surface, you probably have a penetrating sealer. If there is a sheen, flaking, or a slightly plastic feel, there is an acrylic film. If water soaks in and darkens the paver immediately, any previous sealer is mostly gone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based acrylic generally bonds poorly to a sound solvent acrylic film, because the solvent film is slick and non-porous to it. The safe route is to stay with solvent if the old coat is solvent and in good shape, or strip the old coat completely before switching. Water-based over water-based is usually fine after a thorough cleaning. Penetrating repellents cannot soak in through any film, so a film has to come off first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand adds another wrinkle. It needs to cure fully, often several days to a few weeks depending on the brand and weather, before a sealer goes on top, and some poly sand makers specify which sealer types they approve. A water-based sealer is usually the safer choice over poly sand, since strong solvents can soften some polymer binders. Check the sand bag before choosing the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, test. Seal a small, out-of-the-way patch, let it cure for several days, then press a strip of masking tape onto it and pull it off sharply. If flakes come off on the tape, adhesion is poor and you need to strip or switch products. It is a 10-minute test that can save an entire weekend of stripping later. If you are choosing sand as well, see our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand-for-pavers/">polymeric sand rankings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Sealer to Buy and What It Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Label coverage figures assume an average paver. In practice, smooth and dense concrete pavers might cover 150 to 250 square feet per gallon per coat, while tumbled or porous pavers can drop to 80 to 120. Flooding joints for sand stabilization can add 25 percent or more to a first coat. Penetrating repellents are usually applied as one saturating coat and often cover a similar or slightly larger area.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate, measure the patio, take the low end of the label coverage for the first coat, and add a second coat at a slightly better spread rate. Round up to whole pails and buy all of it from the same batch if the product enhances color. Running out halfway and finishing with a different batch can leave a visible line where the color or sheen changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, water-based paver acrylics generally run roughly $30 to $60 per gallon, budget penetrants like Eagle Natural Seal sit at the low end of that range, and premium siloxane and hybrid products run roughly $50 to $100 a gallon. Five-gallon pails usually cut the per-gallon cost meaningfully. Add a pump sprayer, rollers, a cleaner, and possibly joint sand, and a DIY 400-square-foot job lands somewhere around $200 to $500 in materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare cost per year, not per gallon. A film sealer that needs recoating every two years and stripping every six or eight can cost more over a decade than a premium penetrant that lasts much longer per its label. The right choice depends on whether you want the look of a film, but the budget math often favors penetrants for driveways and walks.
          </p>

