        <div id="milky-fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What to Do When the Finish Turns Milky
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cloudy, white or blue-gray cast that appears a few hours to a day after sealing is called blushing, and with water-based acrylics it is almost always moisture: damp pavers, a thick coat, high humidity, or dew on the curing film. Sometimes light blushing clears on its own as the film finishes curing in warm, dry weather. Give it a week of good weather before you do anything drastic.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the haze persists, try the gentle options first. A stiff brush and water on a hot, dry day can sometimes break up surface blush on a thin coat. Some manufacturers sell a reactivating product or recommend a specific solvent to soften their own film; check the maker's guidance rather than guessing, because the wrong solvent can make it worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            When those fail, stripping is the reliable fix. Use a stripper made for acrylic sealers, follow the dwell time, agitate with a stiff brush, and rinse thoroughly. Expect the joint sand to need topping up afterward. Let the pavers dry for several days before resealing, and this time apply thinner coats in better weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevention is cheaper. Seal only after a dry spell, start late morning so the film cures in rising temperatures, keep coats thin, and keep sprinklers off. If you are unsure whether the pavers are dry enough, wait another day. Water-based sealer rewards patience more than any other step in the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Long It Lasts and How to Maintain It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based acrylic films typically last about two to four years on a patio, less in sun-baked spots or traffic lanes, and less on driveways. The first sign is that water stops beading in high-traffic areas, followed by color fading and joint sand starting to wash out again. Penetrating repellents often carry longer service-life claims from their makers, and the giveaway when they fade is water soaking in rather than beading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is simple. Sweep regularly, rinse spills before they set, and clean with a pH-neutral paver cleaner rather than strong acids or bleach, which can dull a film. In spring, check for joint sand loss and weeds and top up the joints before they become a problem. Avoid aiming a pressure washer directly at sealed joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the coating wears, clean the pavers and apply a single thin maintenance coat of the same product. Resist the urge to add a coat every year. Too many layers of acrylic build into a thick film that is more prone to whitening and peeling, and eventually you will need to strip all of it. Every few cycles, consider stripping and starting fresh.
          </p>

          <p>
            In snow country, pick de-icers carefully, since aggressive chlorides speed up wear on both sealer and paver. Our guide to the <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">best ice melt for concrete</a> covers which products are gentler on masonry. For poured slabs elsewhere on the property, the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealer</a> guide is the better resource, since slab sealers are formulated somewhat differently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing on a cool evening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based acrylic needs warmth to coalesce into a clear film, and evening dew lands right when the film is most vulnerable. Start in late morning on a dry day so the coat cures in rising temperatures. If the forecast shows the night dropping toward the forties, push the job to a warmer day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating it like solvent sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent acrylic forgives a bad coat because it can be re-melted. Water-based acrylic generally cannot, so a cloudy or streaky coat usually means stripping. Plan the job as if there is no undo: thin coats, a good weather window, dry pavers, and a test patch before committing to the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding damp joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A joint-stabilizing sealer can only bind sand that is dry and full. Flooding wet or settled joints wastes product and leaves sand that washes out at the next storm. Refill the joints, sweep the faces clean, give everything a full dry day, and then flood the joints on the first coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over an old solvent film</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based sealer often fails to bond to an intact solvent acrylic, leading to peeling within a season. Check what is on the pavers first. Either stay with a compatible solvent product or strip the old coat completely before switching. A masking-tape adhesion test on a small patch confirms compatibility.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing too soon over new poly sand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand needs to cure before a sealer goes on top, and the time varies by brand and weather. Seal too early and you can trap moisture in the joints, haze the joint lines, or weaken the bond. Read the sand bag for the minimum wait and follow it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do water-based paver sealers compare with solvent ones on durability?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For stain resistance and water repellency, the better water-based products come close. Solvent acrylics still tend to give a deeper gloss and are easier to repair because they re-melt. Water-based penetrating repellents are a different story; they are a standard choice on bridges and parking decks and hold up well. Durability depends more on prep, thin coats and weather than on whether the carrier is water or solvent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a water-based sealer produce a wet look?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, though usually a slightly softer one than solvent products. Water-based wet-look acrylics like Foundation Armor WB25 deepen color clearly and add a satin-to-semigloss sheen. Two thin coats generally look better than one heavy coat. If you want the deepest possible glossy look and solvent products are legal where you live, a solvent acrylic will still edge it out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my water-based sealer turn white?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always because of moisture. The pavers were damp when sealed, the coat went on too thick, humidity was high, or dew settled on the film before it cured. Light haze sometimes clears in warm, dry weather over several days. Heavy or lasting haze on a water-based acrylic usually needs stripping and resealing with thinner coats on a better day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats should I apply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based acrylic labels call for two thin coats, with the second applied after the first is dry to the touch and within the recoat window. Penetrating repellents are usually applied as one saturating coat, sometimes wet-on-wet, until the paver stops absorbing. Always follow the specific product's directions, because recoat timing varies between formulas and getting it wrong can affect adhesion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is water-based paver sealer safe around pets and plants?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based sealers have far lower VOCs and odor than solvent products, which makes them a better choice near gardens, pools and pets. They are still chemical coatings, so keep pets and children off the surface until it has cured, avoid overspray on plants, and read the safety data sheet. Once fully cured, a sealed patio poses little practical concern for normal use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will water-based sealer stabilize joint sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A water-based acrylic designed for pavers, such as SB-6000 or Blok-Guard, will bind the top of ordinary joint sand if the joints are full and dry when you flood them. Penetrating repellents do not bind sand. For joints that wash out badly, re-sanding with polymeric sand is the stronger fix, and you can then seal the faces with whichever product suits your finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the coldest temperature for applying it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
